//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: `==`/`!=` between two class-typed values already work TODAY with
//@ reason: ZERO CeeLing-specific operator-overload machinery -- `==`/`!=` are
//@ reason: ordinary comparison operators in binary_expression (same
//@ reason: grammar production as `<`/`>`/etc.), and a class-typed operand is
//@ reason: always smart-pointer-backed, so the comparison compiles as
//@ reason: `std::shared_ptr<T>::operator==`/`!=` (C++'s own built-in pointer-
//@ reason: identity comparison) with no rewriting needed at all -- the exact
//@ reason: "escape by construction" pattern operator*/operator++ on a value
//@ reason: type already established, just via a completely different route
//@ reason: (smart-pointer built-ins, not a user-defined operator).
//@ reason: Motivated by yaml-cpp's `Node::operator==` (== `is()`, IDENTITY
//@ reason: comparison, not structural/value comparison -- confirmed by
//@ reason: reading yaml-cpp's own node/impl.h) needing exactly this
//@ reason: semantics for yaml-cling; see notes/GAPS.md in yaml-cling.
//@ run: same=1 different=1 still usable: 1
//
// A shared alias (via an alias-returning method) compares equal to the
// original; two independently-constructed objects compare unequal even with
// identical field values (IDENTITY, not structural equality) -- and the
// compared values remain fully usable afterward (comparison is a read, never
// a move -- BinaryExpression's existing generic handling already covers
// this, confirmed via this fixture actually running, not just parsing).

class Foo {
public:
    int x;
    Foo(int v) {
        x = v;
    }
    Foo getSelf() {
        return this;
    }
};

int main() {
    Foo a = new Foo(1);
    Foo aAlias = a.getSelf();
    Foo b = new Foo(1);

    bool same = (a == aAlias);
    bool different = (a != b);

    std::cout << "same=" << same << " different=" << different
               << " still usable: " << a.x << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
